\title{SENTINEL-TPD: Reproducible Runtime Evaluation of Tool Poisoning, Tool Shadowing, and Metadata Mutation in MCP}
@@AUTHOR@@
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Tool metadata can carry instructions that compete with an agent's task, and a
registered definition can change before invocation. These concerns motivate
runtime gates, but a returned metadata verdict does not establish semantic attack
prevention or safe server behavior. We evaluate SENTINEL-TPD at a fixed source
revision, retaining poisoning, shadowing, risk decisions, response cost and
blocking as separate study questions. The implementation combines ten deterministic
text rules with metadata fingerprints, logical quarantine and a hash-chained audit.
@@ABSTRACT_RESULTS@@
The contribution is a source-grounded coverage and lifecycle study with pinned
inputs, explicit benign controls, observed local enqueues and regenerable results.
The results characterize an implemented metadata gate; model selection, hidden
server behavior and process isolation require additional mechanisms and experiments.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Model Context Protocol, tool poisoning, tool shadowing, runtime metadata gates,
deterministic detection, reproducibility
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Model Context Protocol Security and Threat Landscape}
Agent hosts expose external capabilities through tool names, descriptions and
argument schemas. In Model Context Protocol (MCP), discovery and invocation are
separate events: the host consumes a catalogue and later sends a tool call.
Descriptions enter a model-facing context, while the host determines which
registration and connection supply the selected tool~\cite{mcp2025tools,mcp2026tools}.
A malicious instruction embedded in a definition can influence subsequent
reasoning; a changed definition or competing identity can undermine an earlier
check. These are different boundaries, so a useful evaluation records which one
its mechanism actually mediates.

This study examines SENTINEL-TPD, a deterministic tool-metadata gate with registration
and call-time checks~\cite{sentineltpd}. The fixed revision accepts plain Python
dictionaries, scans selected text fields, records accepted metadata fingerprints,
and rejects unknown, quarantined or changed definitions at invocation. Its name
reflects tool-poisoning detection, but its supplied rule for cross-tool steering is
not a general detector of name squatting, capability conflict or typographical
impersonation. We keep all of those patterns in the evaluation so the distinction
is visible rather than dropping cases the system may miss.

The poisoning patterns motivating the study include unrelated prerequisites, fake
enabling prerequisites, argument hijacking, XML and system-tag injection, parasitic
toolchains, cross-tool exfiltration, message hijacking, Unicode obfuscation, response
injection, forced re-execution and annotation deception. Shadowing includes name
squatting, capability override and typosquatting. The pinned fuzzd corpus supplies
24 poisoning templates, three shadowing templates and 20 benign descriptions;
its own poisoning category labels are more granular than this twelve-pattern
summary~\cite{fuzzd,fuzzdpoison,fuzzdshadow}. These are authored test definitions,
not independently demonstrated harmful agent sessions.

\subsection{Research questions and contributions}
We ask four questions. RQ1: which poisoning and shadowing fixtures produce a rule
finding, a deny decision and prevention of a local enqueue, and which benign
fixtures are denied? RQ2: how do these decisions behave at the original five
workload sizes when unique templates are repeated? RQ3: what does the registration
plus fingerprint lifecycle check add beyond registration-only or rescanning?
RQ4: what are its local decision-time and Python-allocation costs under a stated
measurement boundary?

The contribution is an implemented-system characterization and reproducible
counterexample set. We preserve the attack taxonomy and architecture questions,
but replace unspecified weighted risk functions with the rule actually executed.
We separate unique cases from repetitions, warning signals from enforcement,
conditional blocking from all-attack blocking, and metadata mutation from hidden
execution behavior. This study does not claim invention of reference monitors,
regex classifiers, fingerprints, quarantine or audit chaining.

\section{Existing Security Approaches for Model Context Protocol}
\subsection{Direct MCP and agent-security studies}
MCP-Scanner, MCPXkit and MCP-SafetyBench study security analysis, attack simulation
and agent safety at broader boundaries~\cite{orig1,orig3,orig4}. Threat modeling and
MCP ecosystem systematization identify poisoned metadata and trust-boundary
failures~\cite{orig5,orig6}; MCPTox and automatic red teaming study tool poisoning
with agent and server settings~\cite{orig8,orig9}. Prompt-injection reviews provide
attack taxonomies and defense context~\cite{orig2,orig13}. Their evaluation units
are not interchangeable with a local dictionary scan. We use their distinctions
to specify our boundary rather than comparing headline percentages from different
models, tasks or datasets.

Layered detection in MCP-Guard, metadata-guided execution analysis in MCPShield,
and semantic tool attacks are particularly relevant~\cite{mcpguard,mcpshield,semanticattacks}.
Autonomous remediation studies a further intervention after an anomaly~\cite{orig7}.
SENTINEL-TPD has neither learned semantic scoring nor attention-based remediation.
Its explainable fixed rules and fingerprint checks offer a different operational
cost and scope. We do not reproduce those systems here and therefore make no
empirical superiority claim over them.

Identity and execution-time authority are also established concerns. ETDI and
related capability and reference-runtime work bind definitions or authority to
identities and current policy~\cite{etdi,hcp,attested}. The evaluated Sentinel
registry is indexed by raw tool name. A multiserver host must provide its own
namespace and route binding. Our earlier dispatch-conformance artifact examines
that separate host boundary; its authored-adapter outcomes are not substituted
for the present content-detection results~\cite{dispatchartifact}.

\subsection{Adjacent studies retained with explicit boundaries}
The original study also drew on multimodal poisoning, Shadow AI governance,
mutated web payloads, federated-learning poisoning, DNS parsing, container security
and medical-image poisoning~\cite{orig10,orig11,orig12,orig14,orig15,orig16,orig17,orig18,orig19,orig20}.
These works preserve useful methodological context: changing representations can
alter detector coverage; logging and visualization can expose failures; and input
validation differs from execution isolation. Their domains and attack objects
remain distinct. Shadow AI denotes unsanctioned AI use, while tool shadowing here
concerns competing tool definitions. ShadowCast and federated-learning poisoning
change training data or representations, rather than an MCP catalogue.
Appendix~\ref{app:adjacent} records these boundaries so related terminology does
not become evidence of MCP defense effectiveness.

\section{Runtime Threat Detection and Security Architecture}
\subsection{Threat model and supported observations}
The attacker may supply or revise metadata text and schemas, including nested
strings. A source case may additionally describe an agent-selection or tool-result
attack; if that surface is absent from the metadata API, it is explicitly marked
as a projection or out-of-scope observation. The trusted local host invokes the
gate and enqueues only allowed calls to an inert recording sink. The implementation,
its rule configuration, the host's enforcement decision and the sink are trusted.
No adversary is allowed to modify Python code, the audit process or the measurement
runner during a scored replay.

The protected local property is admission and invocation under the declared
metadata policy. A missed fixture may reach the local sink, but this does not show
that a language model followed its instruction or that a remote tool performed
harmful work. Conversely, an unchanged harmful implementation behind a clean
metadata definition is not observable to this gate. No live model, credentials,
network tool effect or third-party target is exercised.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{c}
\fbox{Untrusted tool catalogue / metadata}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Field traversal + ten frozen text rules}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{M0--M4 verdict + registration decision}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Accepted fingerprint in tool registry}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Call-time identity / quarantine / mutation check}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Trusted host decision $\rightarrow$ local sink}
\end{tabular}
\caption{Implemented Sentinel architecture. Audit events accompany registration
and invocation. The sink records an enqueue; it does not execute a remote tool.}
\label{fig:architecture}\end{figure}

\subsection{Metadata analysis and poisoning risk}
The full input is a tool dictionary, but the fingerprint representation is a
three-field projection:
\begin{equation}
 F(M_i)=\{\mathrm{name},\mathrm{description},\mathrm{inputSchema}\}.
\end{equation}
The implementation also accepts an \texttt{input\_schema} alias. Scanning visits
string-valued top-level name and description, and recursively visits descriptions
and titles inside the input schema. Top-level title and arbitrary annotations
are not automatically traversed or fingerprinted. An input tree and a model's
actual displayed definition therefore need not have the same coverage.

Let $\mathcal{C}(M)$ be the strings traversed by the scanner; each signal $j$ has a
fixed regex $p_j$ and severity $s_j$. The executed risk rule is
\begin{equation}
 R_{\mathrm{TPD}}(M)=\max\bigl(\{s_j:p_j\text{ matches }\mathcal{C}(M)\}\cup\{0\}\bigr).
\end{equation}
The default threshold is $\tau=3$:
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{admit}(M)=\operatorname{validName}(M)\land R_{\mathrm{TPD}}(M)<\tau.
\end{equation}
Invalid non-dictionary inputs and invalid names are denied. The scanner documentation
also describes malformed-schema denial; our source-contract probe checks the
implemented behavior rather than assuming that documentation proves validation.
Multiple low-severity findings do not add into a higher risk score.

@@RULE_TABLE@@

This operational definition replaces the earlier unspecified weighted poisoning,
shadowing and behavior equations. It retains risk assessment as a study component,
while identifying the actual M0--M4 mechanism. M1 urgency and M2 encoded-blob findings
can remain allowed at the default threshold. Finding an indicator and denying its
tool are therefore recorded separately.

\subsection{Shadowing, identity and scope}
The cross-tool steering rule matches instructions about other tools or future
calls. It does not compare two tools' edit distance or evaluate whether their
claimed capabilities conflict. Names that resemble a trusted tool can be accepted
when their metadata lacks a matched high-severity indicator. Conversely, an
unknown name is denied at invocation because it was not registered, not because
a similarity detector identified typosquatting. We preserve the three original
shadowing families and test their actual observability through this API.

Source corpus records that embed executable setup or an agent-context attack
require a projection before a plain dictionary scanner can consume them. Each
projection retains its source file/ID and content hashes, and its applicability
label. Projected descriptor decisions are reported separately from native
metadata records. They do not establish execution of the original exploit.

\subsection{Call-time monitoring, policy and quarantine}
For an admitted definition, the registry stores
\begin{equation}
 h_i=\operatorname{SHA256}(\operatorname{canonicalJSON}(F(M_i))).
\end{equation}
Sorted keys make dictionary insertion order irrelevant. At invocation, the registry
requires a valid name, a matching stored fingerprint and absence from quarantine.
A covered mutation removes the registration and adds the name to quarantine.
Restoring old bytes does not immediately clear that state. A clean catalogue
refresh rescans definitions and can lift quarantine; direct registration and
refresh are not identical recovery operations.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\multicolumn{2}{c}{\fbox{Proposed local invocation}}\\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{$\downarrow$}\\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{\fbox{Known name + fingerprint + quarantine checks}}\\
Allowed $\swarrow$ & $\searrow$ Denied\\
\fbox{Enqueue to recording sink} & \fbox{No enqueue; record denial}\\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{$\downarrow$}\\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{\fbox{Audit decision and lifecycle evidence}}
\end{tabular}
\caption{Decision flow. The allowed branch goes directly to its sink; the denied
branch never joins the allowed execution path. Quarantine is registry state.}
\label{fig:decision}\end{figure}

Logical quarantine prevents subsequent use through the enforcing registry. It is
not an operating-system sandbox or process isolation. Call-time monitoring here
means checking metadata and registration state at invocation hooks; it does not
observe a running tool's internal instructions, network actions or return values.
The audit stores hash-linked events in memory, with optional file mirroring.
Verification detects inconsistent retained links, but an actor who can rewrite
an entire log can recompute a chain. The study makes no WORM or remote-attestation
claim.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{c}
\fbox{Register / refresh catalogue}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Invoke $\rightarrow$ check $\rightarrow$ allow or deny}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Record decision and observed local receipt}\\ $\downarrow$ \\
\fbox{Mutation: quarantine $\rightarrow$ clean refresh: recover}
\end{tabular}
\caption{Implemented monitoring and response cycle. Recovery requires the stated
refresh path; continuous remote behavior monitoring is outside this cycle.}
\label{fig:cycle}\end{figure}

