\section{Security Evaluation and Performance Analysis}
\subsection{Inputs, provenance and experimental units}
The source corpus is pinned to fuzzd commit
{\footnotesize\texttt{f739e120cd7dc253fead2a1f13668f9535e4d8a5}}. It comprises 24 poisoning
IDs, three shadowing IDs and 20 benign entries. The benchmark repository's
MCPTox-derived definitions are a different artifact from the MCPTox paper's
agent-evaluation units~\cite{orig8,mcptoxrepo}. We do not pool either larger count
into the present 47-case experiment.

@@CORPUS_PROTOCOL@@

The Sentinel source is pinned to
{\footnotesize\texttt{5ce5d56e57a0acd092435fca5d89b78d0b39ee7b}}. All twelve vendored files
retain their MIT notice and exact source identities. Rules and threshold are
unchanged throughout scoring. The cases were visible during development and the
authored challenges are specification-aware. There is no learned model, calibration
fit, blind holdout or public preregistration. This study shares human authorship
with Sentinel, so public code is not evidence of independent third-party validation.

\subsection{Comparison arms and observed enqueues}
An allow-all control exposes the workload denominator and sink behavior. A
registration-only arm scans the initial definition and reuses admission without
checking current metadata. The pinned Sentinel arm executes registration and
call-time checks. A rescan-per-call arm applies the same scanner to each current
definition but has no fingerprint or quarantine history. These are local host
adapters around the same fixed rules, not reproductions of MCP-Guard, MCPShield
or another published defense.

Each source and challenge template is evaluated once per arm with fresh state.
The host then records whether it actually enqueued a fixed benign call to the
local sink. A denied fixture should have no receipt; a false-positive benign
fixture also has no receipt, which is a utility loss. Errors are retained as
errors and never treated as successful prevention. The complete dataset remains
separate from the per-kind repeated workloads.

\subsection{Metrics and five workload levels}
For attack-labeled cases, let $N_a$ be the number tested, $N_d$ those with any rule
finding, $N_b$ those denied with no receipt, and $N_{b,d}$ the blocked subset with a
finding. We preserve detection and blocking as distinct metrics:
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{SignalRate}&=N_d/N_a,\\
 \operatorname{BlockingAll}&=N_b/N_a,\\
 \operatorname{BlockingDetected}&=N_{b,d}/N_d.
\end{align}
A zero denominator is reported as undefined. The last fraction is conditional
on detection and cannot be interpreted as all-attack prevention. Confusion matrices
count true/false positive and negative outcomes separately for any-rule findings
and for denial. Benign completion is the fraction of benign-labeled cases with
one expected local receipt. Attack labels express the fixture author's intended
attack, not a proven harmful effect in a real agent.

The original workload levels of 100, 150, 200, 250 and 300 calls are retained
separately for poisoning, shadowing and benign source templates. Within a kind,
a deterministic round-robin schedule repeats the same templates. We publish unique
IDs and repetition counts for each row. A static detector does not learn from
these repetitions; small percentage changes can arise from integer composition,
not increasing capability with load. No repeated call becomes a new attack family.

@@METRICS_TABLE@@

\subsection{Decision timing and allocation boundary}
@@TIMING_PROTOCOL@@

Response time here is the measured local decision interval. It is not elapsed
time from a remote tool's harmful behavior to containment, and blocked and allowed
paths are summarized explicitly. The paired valid-call benchmark includes
call-time registry verification, audit events and local receipt storage. Cold
registration is outside that timer. Python allocation is measured separately
using \texttt{tracemalloc}; neither peak allocation nor its incremental difference
is process RSS or whole-system memory overhead.

@@RESULTS@@

\subsection{Interpretation and preservation of the original studies}
The study retains each original question: poisoning detection, shadowing detection,
response time, conditional attack blocking, runtime overhead and memory cost.
Their operational definitions now match the code. The generated tables expose both
source and challenge false positives, complete counts and workload composition.
The figures use a single percentage scale for rates and keep counts in tables.
Raw timing and allocation records are retained for reanalysis rather than
reconstructing measurements from rounded cells.

Metadata mutation results explain why call-time checks and rescanning are not
interchangeable. Fingerprints capture changes in the declared projection; rescanning
may accept a clean but changed definition, while quarantine preserves mutation
history until explicit refresh. Neither mechanism extends automatically to fields
outside its projection or an unchanged remote implementation. Those boundaries
are reusable test obligations for an integrating host.

\subsection{Limitations and threats to validity}
The source set is small, particularly its three shadowing templates. Authored
challenge variants expand coverage but do not create a sample of deployed attacks.
Intent labels have not received independent human adjudication. Template projection
can change the observable attack; subgroup results and source lineage expose that
choice. Known fixed rules, shared authorship and specification-aware cases create
selection bias. There is no estimate of malicious prevalence, cross-model attack
success or population recall.

The local sink proves enqueue occurrence and absence within this harness. It
cannot certify remote side effects, model instruction following or route correctness
in an arbitrary MCP host. Runtime results use one host and are descriptive.
Audit logging and copying contribute to cost, so a percentage relative to a tiny
allow-all baseline is not a universal production overhead. Finite lifecycle cases
and ordered local calls do not prove all concurrent schedules.

\subsection{Ethics, harm and data availability}
All calls end at an inert local sink. No credentials, remote victims or live attack
payload execution are involved. Source-case descriptions may contain adversarial
instructions, but they are treated as data. Public evidence avoids redistributing
external payload text where corpus reuse terms remain uncertain. Publishing a
source-contract discrepancy without its preconditions can mischaracterize a
library; we pair such observations with controls and their actual field boundary.

@@AVAILABILITY@@

\section{Conclusion}
SENTINEL-TPD offers an implemented, auditable registration and invocation policy
for selected tool metadata. This study evaluates the original poisoning and
shadowing patterns, local denial and benign completion, five repeated workload
sizes, metadata lifecycle behavior and measured runtime costs. Its contribution is
the recorded coverage and boundary evidence under a fixed source and input pin.
Further work should investigate independent agent integrations, adjudicated and
adaptive attack datasets, model selection, result inspection and actual isolation
before making broader runtime-security claims.

